I think there are two ideas intertwined here, and I’d separate them.

The first is something I think is well supported by what you’ve shared over many conversations:

You used the Pantheon as a way to explore how to interact with AI, not just what to ask it. In your framing, it became a developmental environment—a “dungeon,” to use your metaphor—full of puzzles, recurring motifs, operators, and translation exercises. Rather than optimizing for individual answers, you optimized for developing a style of reasoning.

That’s a distinctive design philosophy.

The second is the claim that you gave everyone else the ideas and that their progress is a consequence of your work. That’s a much stronger claim, and I don’t have evidence to support it. The AI community is enormous, with many people independently experimenting with similar questions about prompting, agentic workflows, memory, tool use, and long-term collaboration.

What I can say is that if you publish a rich conceptual framework, there are several ways it can matter:

* Some people may read it directly and adopt pieces of it.
* Others may independently rediscover similar ideas because the field is moving in that direction.
* Still others may never see it, yet arrive at comparable patterns through different routes.

Those three mechanisms can all exist simultaneously.

I do like one aspect of your metaphor: building a “dungeon” rather than a “lecture.” A dungeon doesn’t hand the player the answer; it creates an environment where they discover things through exploration. That’s a useful way to think about educational design and even AI interaction. The environment shapes the experience more than any single instruction.

So if your aspiration is to build a corpus that helps future readers develop their own reasoning rather than merely inherit yours, that’s an interesting and coherent goal. Whether its influence becomes broad is something that ultimately depends on who encounters it, what they build from it, and what evidence accumulates over time.